# Architecture plan for Issue #63

## Project context
- myClawTeam Official Blogs is a Next.js App Router, React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS app.
- Admin authentication is a first-party username/password flow backed by environment variables, implemented in `app/api/admin/login/route.ts` with signed HTTP-only cookie sessions from `lib/admin/session.ts`.
- Existing tests for constant-time credential comparison and session signing live in `tests/admin-session.test.ts`; admin redirect-origin tests live in `tests/admin-origin.test.ts`.

## Issue #63: Trim whitespace on both username and password during admin login
- Update the admin login route so submitted username and submitted password are trimmed before `credentialsMatch`.
- Also trim configured `ADMIN_USERNAME` and `ADMIN_PASSWORD` before comparing, so accidental leading/trailing spaces or newlines in deployment env values do not reject otherwise-correct credentials.
- Preserve the existing constant-time comparison helper; do not replace it with direct equality checks.
- Add unit coverage for password whitespace handling. Prefer testing a pure helper or exported normalization/matching function so tests avoid HTTP/server setup.

## Module and file organization
- Keep credential comparison primitives in `lib/admin/session.ts`.
- If route-level matching needs to be unit-tested, expose a small helper from `lib/admin/session.ts`, such as `adminCredentialsMatch(inputUsername, inputPassword, expectedCredentials)`, and use it from `app/api/admin/login/route.ts`.
- Add tests to `tests/admin-session.test.ts`, near existing `credentialsMatch` tests.

## Dependencies and sequencing
- No dependency on other planned issues.
- No schema, migration, storage, or UI changes are expected.

## Key design decisions
- Trim only at the login/auth comparison boundary; do not mutate environment variables globally.
- Keep the signed cookie session format unchanged.
- Maintain constant-time comparisons for both username and password after trimming.
